Introduction to LifeCycle Component:

Like constructors, there are other such methods called automatically at the different levels or phases of component's Life Cycle.They are 
called life cycle methods or life cycle hooks

Phases of Component Life Cycle:

constructor
1. ngOnChanges
2. ngDoCheck
3. ngAfterContentInit
4. ngAfterContentChecked
5. ngAfterViewInit
6. ngAfterViewChecked
7. ngDestroy

1. ngOnChanges
 ->> ngOnChanges hook is called everytime whenever there is a change detected in input properties of the compoenent.
 ->> It is only applicable for @input change
 ->> It cannot be used for other properties.
 ->> simpleChanges is an interface object that is used to provide previous and current values.

@Input() used for passing the value from the parent component to the child compoenent.

2. ngOnInit
 ->> It is only called once when compoenent is initialized, and only once in compoenent life cycle.
 ->> ngOnInit is  called after ngOnChanges life cycle hook.
 ->> ngOnInit is a prefered place to fetch data from an API, but not cpnstructor.

Note: constructor is initialized before component is fully intialized. So it is better to initialize things inside ngOnInit

3. ngDoCheck
 ->> It is used for performing a custom change detection and responding to the changes in a component. It allows to implement our change 
 detection algorithm for the given  component.
 ->> When ngOnChanges hook failed to detect changes in an input property, then we can go for ngDoCheck hook to detect changes in @input
  property.
Note: Never use ngOnChanges and ngDocheck together in the same component, it causes unwanted behaviour.
 ->> ngDoCheck() also helpful to changes in non-input properties.

4. ngAfterContentInit
  ->> It is invoked whenever there is some content projected into the component.
  ->> If we want to send data from parent component to child compoenent it can be done through @input, but it is not possible with html
  tags and css classes to pass from parent compoennet to child component. So, content projection comes into picture.
  ->> content projection is a way to pass the HTML or CSS content from the parent component to child component by using
  <ng-content> directive.
  ->> ngAfterContentInit() hook is invoked whereever there is content projection.
  ->> called Only once throughout the components LifeCycle.
  ->> It is mostly used in relation with the child components and content projection.
  ->> This is called after ngDoCheck() hook.

5. ngAfterContentChecked
 ->> It checks for the content of the component on every change detection cycle.
 ->> This hook will get invoked each time the content of the component is checked after being projected.

6. ngAfterViewInit
 ->> It is called when the angular finishes the initialization of the view( view means A component and its related HTML template ) in the DOM.

7. ngAfterViewChecked
 ->> Angular fires the ngAfterViewChecked() hook after checking the component's view and the child views

8. ngDestroy
 ->> It is called only once during the components lifecycle right before the component gets destroyed.
 ->> Any clean up code for the component should be written inside this hook before the component gets destroyed. 
 ->> The ngOnDestroy() is not called when the user refreshes or closes the browser
 clean up code may include :
 * Removal of local storage information
 * stop timers
 * Tasks that prevent the memory leaks etc...

